# Routh I.Art.89 — oblique impact of equal smooth spheres: the velocities come
# out PERPENDICULAR — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Art. 89: "Two smooth homogeneous spheres A and B impinge obliquely
# on each other. To find the subsequent motion."
#
# THE METHOD. Routh's rule is to resolve along and perpendicular to the LINE
# OF CENTRES at the instant of contact:
#
#   * the spheres are SMOOTH, so no tangential impulse can act, and the
#     tangential component of each velocity is UNCHANGED;
#   * along the line of centres the problem is the direct impact of Art. 83,
#     so momentum plus Newton's restitution rule (Art. 84) settle it.
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE FOR EQUAL ELASTIC SPHERES. With equal masses and e = 1 the
# direct impact exchanges the normal components (Art. 85). So if B is at rest
# and A arrives with velocity v, then writing n for the unit vector along the
# line of centres,
#
#     A keeps   v - (v.n)n        (its tangential part)
#     B takes         (v.n)n      (the whole normal part)
#
# Their scalar product is [v - (v.n)n] . [(v.n)n] = (v.n)(v.n) - (v.n)^2*|n|^2
# = 0 identically. So after ANY oblique elastic impact of equal spheres the
# two velocities are at RIGHT ANGLES -- whatever the impact parameter, whatever
# the speed. (The degenerate exception is the head-on case, where A stops and
# has no direction left.)
#
# THE NUMBERS. Equal unit masses, radius 0.5, restitution 1. B sits at the
# origin; A starts at (-3, 0.5, 0) moving (1, 0, 0), so the impact parameter is
# 0.5 -- half the contact separation, giving a 30-degree oblique hit. Contact
# occurs when the centres are 1 apart, i.e. at A_x = -sqrt(0.75) =
# -0.8660254037844386, reached at t = 2.1339745962155614. The line of centres
# there is n = (-0.8660254037844386, 0.5, 0), and v.n = -0.8660254037844386, so
#
#     A after = (0.25,  0.4330127018922193, 0)     |A| = 0.5
#     B after = (0.75, -0.4330127018922193, 0)     |B| = 0.8660254037844386
#
# Checks the cells print: their dot product is 0 (perpendicular); the momentum
# sum is (1, 0, 0), unchanged; and 0.5^2 + 0.8660254^2 = 1, so vis viva is
# conserved exactly, as it must be for e = 1.
#
# SETUP NOTE. Gravity is off (g_constant = 0) -- posim's pairwise gravity is on
# by default with G = 1 and would bend the approach before contact.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a sphere clips another off-centre and the two leave along
# paths that make a perfect right angle -- the shape every billiard player
# knows, here as a consequence of Routh's two resolutions.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_oblique_impact
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_oblique_impact.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_oblique_impact.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
collide on

new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [-3, 0.5, 0], velocity = [1, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [ 0, 0,   0], restitution = 1 }

momentum
energy

run 4 steps 4

obj0.velocity
obj1.velocity
# the two velocities are perpendicular
dot(obj0.velocity, obj1.velocity)
# speeds 0.5 and 0.8660254037844386, momentum and vis viva both unchanged
norm(obj0.velocity)
norm(obj1.velocity)
momentum
energy
system.collisions
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
collide on
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [-3, 0.5, 0], velocity = [1, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [ 0, 0,   0], restitution = 1 }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.02
